  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss over x, z from -2 to 2. x (left-right) is the steep direction. Plain steps flip and grow there when the learning rate is above 0.8.",
    "Step = -rate x (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 1.5 x sqrt((64-b)/(63 b)) on each slope component. That is 1.5 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. A fixed seed sets it, and New run continues the sequence.",
    "Both arrows show steps (rate x slope estimate), drawn at 3 times their true length on one shared scale. Purple: the next mini-batch step. Dark: the full-data step, against the true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 5, the loss at the bowl's steep edge (diverged). Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. The Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1x speed. Changing a setting affects the next step."
  ]
}